## j-FAMILY (double-width compare) — COMPLETE (chunk 4)
CONFIRMED by the gate at the j=1 cells: D616<590> 2 bad, D616<615> 3 bad, D924<693> 1 bad,
all Ceiling, classified wrong-mode(HalfToEven). Live defect in released 0.5.0; issue #73.

The fix compares delta^2 <= 2*D*one at DOUBLE width because delta^2 overflows a single width.
At j=1 it does NOT overflow (D616<615>: delta^2 = 10^616 vs Int<32> max ~1.63*10^616), so the
double-width path was exercised by nothing. These rows force it.
Window on L = log10(k), all three must hold:
  overflow   L > log10(2^(64N-1))/2      (else single width would have sufficed)
  exposure   L < S - (reach - log10 3)/3 (else the walker resolves the cubic)
  capturable L >= S - (1233 - log10 3)/3 (else the .au truncates it -> FALSE NEGATIVE)
Qualifying driven cells and the k used (k^2/2 * 10^-S is an exact integer in every case):
  D462<461>  window (231.0, 293.2)  k = 10^232  d = 10^-229  delta^2 = 83x max
  D616<590>  window (308.1, 336.8)  k = 10^315  d = 10^-275  delta^2 = 6.2e13x max
  D616<615>  window (308.1, 361.8)  k = 10^328  d = 10^-287  delta^2 = 6.2e39x max
  D924<900>  window (489.2, 561.5)  k = 10^525  d = 10^-375  delta^2 = 3.4e125x max
  D924<923>  window (512.2, 584.5)  k = 10^548  d = 10^-375  delta^2 = 3.4e171x max
  (D924<900> and <923> share the same input string; one row serves both cells.)
NO QUALIFYING CELL, stated as findings not gaps:
  D307 and every narrower tier — overflow needs L > 154.0 but exposure caps L < 138.2 at the
    widest driven cell (D307<306>). The windows are disjoint at EVERY scale, not just the
    driven ones: storage is too narrow relative to reach, so a delta big enough to overflow
    the width is always big enough for the walker to see its cubic. Structural, not a grid gap.
  D1232 — same wall as before: capturability needs L >= 789.2 at S=1200, exposure needs
    L < 733.5. Disjoint. D1232 remains unreachable by golden data for this family entirely.
SIDE RESULT: larger k pulls the cubic SHALLOWER (depth 3(S - log10 k)), which is what brings
D924<900>/<923> inside gen_precision. At j=1 they were uncapturable (depth 1350/1383). The
j-family therefore reaches cells j=1 provably could not — it is not merely "easier rows".

## Why the BELOW-1 side is clean under Floor (the asymmetry, explained)
Answered from `tang_ln_fixed_g` Stage 1 (ln_tang.rs:174-188), v = 2^k * m with m in [1,2):
  x = 1 + d : v >= one_w, so k = 0 and m = v. There is NO ln2 term. The artanh series on a
              tiny argument reproduces d - d^2/2 exactly at working scale, so the value lands
              EXACTLY on a grid multiple, rem == 0, residual_present == false, and the directed
              bump cannot fire. -> BREAKS under Ceiling.
  x = 1 - d : v < one_w, so the loop's `m < one_w => k -= 1` forces k = -1 and m = 2(1-d).
              The result is -ln2 + ln(2-2d). ln2 is a ROUNDED working-scale constant, so that
              subtraction leaves a generically NONZERO low residual, rem != 0, and Floor's
              bump (residual_present && !result_positive) fires -> lands on the correct
              -(k+Q)-1. Correct, but for a reason unrelated to the deficit it is masking.
CONCLUSION: no CONSTRUCTIBLE below-1 input mis-rounds under Floor in this family. A break needs
the -ln2 + ln(2-2d) working-scale value to land exactly on a grid multiple, i.e. controlling the
rounding of an irrational to `guard` digits — that IS the Table-Maker's Dilemma, not something a
derivation can target. Recorded as UNBROKEN-AND-EXPLAINED, NOT proven safe: the below-1
correctness is UNEARNED (it rests on a residual that is nonzero only generically). LATENT HAZARD
worth keeping: any future change that removes the ln2 subtraction near 1 — an exact-ln2 path, or
a reduction that keeps k = 0 below 1 — would expose the below-1 bracket exactly as the above-1
bracket was exposed. The fix's below-1 branch is correct but its FIRING path stays unexercised.

